From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hood Chatham <roberthoodchatham@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] Add PyUnstable_ExecStack API for stackful coroutine
 libraries

Squashed from the execstack-3.14 branch of CPython. An execution stack
is the per-coroutine part of a PyThreadState (frame chain and datastack,
exception stack, contextvars context, recursion/tracing depth, ...).
PyUnstable_ExecStack_Suspend()/Resume()/Switch() move that state in and
out of the thread state; Traverse()/Clear() let the owner participate in
GC. Entry frames on the C stack are unlinked while a stack is suspended
so its frames are safe to introspect. Pyodide's stack switching uses
this instead of poking PyThreadState fields.

The Makefile.pre.in and _testcapi hunks are included so the CPython test
suite for the API (Lib/test/test_capi/test_execstack.py, not included)
could be run; the ucontext fiber is only compiled on Linux.
---
diff --git a/Include/cpython/pystate.h b/Include/cpython/pystate.h
index f428396411c..a0a13cd21a2 100644
--- a/Include/cpython/pystate.h
+++ b/Include/cpython/pystate.h
@@ -255,6 +255,101 @@ PyAPI_FUNC(int) PyUnstable_ThreadState_SetStackProtection(
 PyAPI_FUNC(void) PyUnstable_ThreadState_ResetStackProtection(
     PyThreadState *tstate);
 
+/* Execution stacks (unstable API).
+
+   An execution stack is the part of a thread state that belongs to one
+   stackful coroutine: the chain of interpreter frames and the memory they
+   live in, the exception-handling stack, the contextvars context, recursion
+   and tracing depth, and a few related bookkeeping fields. Libraries that
+   implement stackful coroutines by switching C stacks (greenlet, Pyodide)
+   use this API to swap that state in and out of a PyThreadState without
+   touching its fields directly.
+
+   The thread state does not track execution stacks itself. The library
+   creates one with PyUnstable_ExecStack_New() for every coroutine and one
+   with PyUnstable_ExecStack_Adopt() for the thread's original stack, and
+   names them explicitly when switching. A stack is either running (its
+   state is live in the thread state) or not (its state, if it has any yet,
+   is saved in the stack object).
+
+   The error indicator (tstate->current_exception) is *not* part of an
+   execution stack: it travels with control across a switch, which is how a
+   library throws an exception into a stack. */
+
+// Feature test macro for extensions that support older Pythons too.
+#define Py_HAVE_EXECSTACK 1
+
+typedef struct _PyExecStack PyUnstable_ExecStack;
+
+// Create a stack with no frames and an empty contextvars context (use
+// SetContext() to give it one). It is not running; the first Switch() into
+// it starts it with an empty frame chain. Returns NULL with MemoryError on
+// failure.
+PyAPI_FUNC(PyUnstable_ExecStack *) PyUnstable_ExecStack_New(void);
+
+// Create a running stack standing for whatever is executing on tstate right
+// now (typically called once per thread, for the thread's original stack).
+// Does not touch the thread state. Returns NULL with MemoryError on failure.
+PyAPI_FUNC(PyUnstable_ExecStack *) PyUnstable_ExecStack_Adopt(
+    PyThreadState *tstate);
+
+// Move the per-stack state out of tstate into `current`, which must be the
+// stack that is running on tstate, and install `target`, which must not be
+// running and, if it has run before, must have run on tstate. A no-op if the
+// two are the same. Returns -1 with an exception set and the thread state
+// unchanged on failure.
+//
+// Call this *before* switching C stacks, while attached to tstate and while
+// the outgoing C stack is still intact: the interpreter reads the frames and
+// critical sections it keeps there. It does not read the incoming C stack, so
+// it is fine if that has not been restored yet (as in greenlet, where the two
+// share the same memory). No Python code may run between this call and the
+// completion of the C stack switch. Does not run Python code itself.
+PyAPI_FUNC(int) PyUnstable_ExecStack_Switch(
+    PyThreadState *tstate,
+    PyUnstable_ExecStack *current,
+    PyUnstable_ExecStack *target);
+
+// 1 if the stack's state is live in a thread state, 0 if it is saved in the
+// stack object (or the stack has not run yet).
+PyAPI_FUNC(int) PyUnstable_ExecStack_IsRunning(PyUnstable_ExecStack *stack);
+
+// Return a new reference to the topmost complete frame of the stack, or NULL
+// (with the error indicator cleared) if there is none. Same contract as
+// PyThreadState_GetFrame(). Safe to call on a stack that is not running;
+// f_back on the result is also safe. A running stack's frames live in its
+// thread state and can only be read from that thread; from any other thread
+// this returns NULL.
+PyAPI_FUNC(PyFrameObject *) PyUnstable_ExecStack_GetFrame(
+    PyUnstable_ExecStack *stack);
+
+// Contextvars context of the stack. GetContext returns a new reference or
+// NULL. SetContext accepts NULL or a PyContext. For a running stack these
+// operate on its thread state and must be called from that thread: GetContext
+// returns NULL and SetContext fails with SystemError from any other thread.
+PyAPI_FUNC(PyObject *) PyUnstable_ExecStack_GetContext(
+    PyUnstable_ExecStack *stack);
+PyAPI_FUNC(int) PyUnstable_ExecStack_SetContext(
+    PyUnstable_ExecStack *stack,
+    PyObject *context);
+
+// For the owner's tp_traverse. Visits the objects owned by a stack that is
+// not running, including the contents of its frames.
+PyAPI_FUNC(int) PyUnstable_ExecStack_Traverse(
+    PyUnstable_ExecStack *stack,
+    visitproc visit,
+    void *arg);
+
+// For the owner's tp_clear. Release everything the stack owns, clearing any
+// suspended frames. The stack remains allocated but can no longer be switched
+// to. Returns -1 with SystemError if the stack is running on this thread.
+PyAPI_FUNC(int) PyUnstable_ExecStack_Clear(PyUnstable_ExecStack *stack);
+
+// Clear (unless running, in which case the live state belongs to the thread)
+// and deallocate. Stacks are not reference counted: the library owns each one.
+// A stack whose thread has exited can still be inspected, cleared and freed.
+PyAPI_FUNC(void) PyUnstable_ExecStack_Free(PyUnstable_ExecStack *stack);
+
 /* Routines for advanced debuggers, requested by David Beazley.
    Don't use unless you know what you are doing! */
 PyAPI_FUNC(PyInterpreterState *) PyInterpreterState_Main(void);
